{"action_id":"a0004","additional_user_input":[],"audience":"unspecified","constraints":["Do not propose computational checks; return an empty proposed_checks list."],"context":null,"goal":null,"inputs":{"branches":{"a":{"answer":"There are 7 subsets:\n{1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.\n\nFor example, {1,8} has sum 9. Exhaustiveness follows by cardinality: no singleton is possible because 9 is absent; the four two-element cases and three three-element cases are exactly those listed; and any subset of four or more distinct positive elements has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10.","approaches":[{"description":"Enumerate qualifying subsets by increasing cardinality, always writing their elements in increasing order.","id":"cardinality-case-split","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It produces a complete finite case split and an explicit lower bound rules out every cardinality at least four."},{"description":"List arbitrary subsets first and retain those whose sums equal 9.","id":"unstructured-subset-listing","outcome":"rejected","reason":"It can find the answer but does not by itself give a concise, checkable exhaustiveness argument."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question specifies subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} with sum 9.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"problem-domain","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The objects under consideration are subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} whose element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the cardinality cases in steps singleton-case through large-cardinality-case.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["problem-domain"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"complete-enumeration","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The qualifying subsets are exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","step_ids":["singleton-case","pair-case","triple-case","large-cardinality-case"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The seven distinct subsets in complete-enumeration are the complete list.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["complete-enumeration"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"count-seven","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets have sum 9.","step_ids":["count-listed-subsets"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct addition.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["complete-enumeration"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"exhibited-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is one qualifying subset, since 1+8=9.","step_ids":["exhibit-subset"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"singleton-case","justification":"A singleton with sum 9 would have to be {9}, but 9 is not an element of the specified ground set.","statement":"There are no qualifying singleton subsets."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"pair-case","justification":"For an increasing pair {a,b}, a+b=9. Taking a=1,2,3,4 gives respectively b=8,7,6,5. If a>=5, then b>a implies a+b>=5+6>9. Thus these four are all increasing pairs.","statement":"The qualifying two-element subsets are exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, and {4,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"triple-case","justification":"Write an increasing triple as {a,b,c}. If a=1, then b+c=8 with 1<b<c, giving {1,2,6} and {1,3,5}; b=4 would force c=4, not distinct. If a=2, then b+c=7 with 2<b<c, giving {2,3,4}. If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12>9. Hence the list is complete.","statement":"The qualifying three-element subsets are exactly {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"large-cardinality-case","justification":"Any subset with at least four distinct positive elements has sum at least the sum of the four least available elements, 1+2+3+4=10, which exceeds 9.","statement":"No qualifying subset has four or more elements."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["singleton-case","pair-case","triple-case","large-cardinality-case"],"id":"count-listed-subsets","justification":"The cardinality cases are disjoint and exhaustive. They contain 0, 4, 3, and 0 qualifying subsets respectively, for a total of 7.","statement":"The complete enumeration contains exactly seven subsets."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"exhibit-subset","justification":"Both 1 and 8 belong to the ground set, are distinct, and 1+8=9.","statement":"{1,8} qualifies."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]},"b":{"answer":"There are 8 subsets. One is {8,1}. Exhaustively classifying a subset by its largest element m gives: m=8: {8,1}; m=7: {7,2}; m=6: {6,3}, {6,2,1}; m=5: {5,4}, {5,3,1}; m=4: {4,3,2}. No largest element below 4 can work, since 1+2+3=6. Thus the list is exhaustive and has 8 members.","approaches":[{"description":"Classify each candidate subset by its largest element and solve for the remaining sum using smaller distinct elements.","id":"largest-element-casework","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The cases m=4,5,6,7,8 are disjoint and cover every possible subset summing to 9."},{"description":"Use a generating function and read the coefficient of x^9 in the product from i=1 to 8 of (1+x^i).","id":"generating-function","outcome":"incomplete","reason":"This would give the same count, but the largest-element argument already proves the coefficient count directly without an expansion."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the exhaustive largest-element case split in steps classify-maximum through total-count.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"eight-subsets","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly eight subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9.","step_ids":["classify-maximum","case-eight","case-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four","total-count"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Established in step case-eight.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{8,1} is one subset with sum 9.","step_ids":["case-eight"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"classify-maximum","justification":"Any subset with sum 9 has a unique largest element m. Since every element is positive and belongs to {1,...,8}, m is at most 8. If m is at most 3, the subset sum is at most 1+2+3=6; hence m is in {4,5,6,7,8}.","statement":"It suffices to consider the five disjoint cases m=4,5,6,7,8."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["classify-maximum"],"id":"case-eight","justification":"The remaining elements must sum to 9-8=1, and the only subset of {1,...,7} with sum 1 is {1}.","statement":"For m=8, the sole subset is {8,1}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["classify-maximum"],"id":"case-seven","justification":"The remaining elements must sum to 2, and the only subset of {1,...,6} with sum 2 is {2}.","statement":"For m=7, the sole subset is {7,2}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["classify-maximum"],"id":"case-six","justification":"The remaining elements must sum to 3. The distinct-element subsets of {1,...,5} summing to 3 are {3} and {2,1}.","statement":"For m=6, the subsets are {6,3} and {6,2,1}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["classify-maximum"],"id":"case-five","justification":"The remaining elements must sum to 4. The distinct-element subsets of {1,2,3,4} summing to 4 are {4} and {3,1}.","statement":"For m=5, the subsets are {5,4} and {5,3,1}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["classify-maximum"],"id":"case-four","justification":"The remaining elements must sum to 5 using only {1,2,3}. Its total is 6, so such a subset must omit elements of total 1; it therefore omits only 1 and is {2,3}.","statement":"For m=4, the sole subset is {4,3,2}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["case-ei